# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitattributes — how git should treat the bytes in these files.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: git has a per-machine setting, `core.autocrlf`. On Windows
# it is commonly `true`, which means "store LF in the repository, but write CRLF
# into my working directory on checkout". It is not shared between clones, so
# every contributor and every CI runner may have it set differently.
#
# Two things go wrong without this file, and neither one looks like a
# line-endings problem when you hit it:
#
#   1. Phantom diffs. Someone changes one line, and `git diff` shows all 800 lines
#      of the file as modified, because the endings were rewritten too. The real
#      change becomes unreviewable.
#
#   2. Broken containers. This project ships a Dockerfile. A shell script or
#      entrypoint checked out with CRLF fails inside a Linux image with
#      `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M` — a message that sends people looking at the
#      wrong thing entirely, because the file looks correct in every editor.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git: treat these as text, normalise to LF on commit,
# AND check them out as LF regardless of what `core.autocrlf` says locally. The
# bytes on disk then match the bytes in the repository on every platform.
#
# WHY HERE RATHER THAN A GIT SETTING: .gitattributes is committed, so it applies
# to everyone who clones, including CI. A `git config` fix works on exactly one
# machine and has to be remembered again on the next one. Configuration that must
# hold for the project belongs in the project.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so git never attempts line-ending conversion on them.
# Rewriting bytes inside an image that happened to look like a CRLF corrupts it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.db binary
